# ============================================================================
# public/_headers
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOST-LEVEL SECURITY HEADERS (Cloudflare Pages / Netlify format).
#
# WHY here and not in next.config: a static-export site has no Next.js server to
# run the headers() function, so security headers are applied by the HOST. This
# file is honored by Cloudflare Workers Static Assets (the mechanism that serves
# this site) as well as by Cloudflare Pages and Netlify, applying these headers
# to every asset response. Verified live: every header below is present on
# production responses. It is the static-export equivalent of the canon Red/Blue
# (RB-01…RB-07) hardening — the protection is identical, just applied at the edge.
#
# When deployed to a different host, port these headers to that host's mechanism.
#
# TWO LINES IN THE OVERHEAD (PRIME, 2026-10-04). After the security headers, every
# response also carries X-Clacks-Overhead: GNU Terry Pratchett, the memorial
# header thousands of sites have sent since March 2015 (the clacks of Going
# Postal: G, pass it on; N, do not log it; U, turn it around at the end of the
# line), and X-Collective, a stated fact about method dressed as a joke: good
# ideas are adopted here and credited. Neither does anything; both cost a few
# dozen bytes; both are addressed to the people who read headers. The story and
# the sources are in the Learn article hidden-messages-in-http-headers.
#
# SCRIPT-SRC NOTE: script-src intentionally includes 'unsafe-inline' and
# 'wasm-unsafe-eval'. This is REQUIRED by the chosen architecture, not a lapse:
#   - 'unsafe-inline': Next.js App Router (static export) emits per-page inline
#     RSC hydration scripts (self.__next_f.push(...)). They cannot be hashed
#     (content differs on every page, so no fixed hash list works in a global
#     header) nor nonced (a static export has no server to mint per-request
#     nonces). Without this, React cannot hydrate and the site renders blank.
#   - 'wasm-unsafe-eval': Pagefind compiles its search index as client-side
#     WebAssembly, which CSP gates behind this token.
# Residual risk is low on a static, first-party site with no server-side
# reflection of user input into HTML and React-escaped tool output (no inline
# injection vector). A stricter nonce-based CSP would require a Worker injecting
# per-request nonces via HTMLRewriter (a documented future hardening option),
# not something a static _headers file can do.
# ============================================================================

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  X-Clacks-Overhead: GNU Terry Pratchett
  X-Collective: resistance is futile; good ideas are assimilated here, with their sources (see /colophon)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Explicit content types for the machine-readable outputs (2026-07-28).
#
# PRIME observed that /sitemap.xml "does not appear to be in XML format" in a
# browser. The file IS well-formed XML - it opens with an XML declaration and a
# correct urlset namespace - but nothing here declared its type, and the
# `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` above means the browser will NOT infer one.
# So it arrives as an undeclared blob and is rendered as plain text.
#
# Declaring the type fixes the display and, more importantly, makes the file
# unambiguous to anything that checks the header before parsing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/sitemap.xml
  Content-Type: application/xml; charset=utf-8

/feed.xml
  Content-Type: application/rss+xml; charset=utf-8

/llms.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The WebAssembly engines and their worker scripts (2026-10-04).
#
# /wasm/<engine>/<version>/ holds the files a WebAssembly tool downloads on
# first use (for Pyodide: 13.5 MB in five files, copied at prebuild from the
# lockfile-pinned npm package, digests verified by scripts/check-wasm-engines).
# The version is in the path, so the files can be cached for a year and marked
# immutable: a new engine is a new path, never a changed file. The second visit
# costs nothing, which is what the page promises.
#
# Content types are declared for the same reason as /sitemap.xml above: with
# nosniff set, a module script served as anything but JavaScript is REFUSED
# by the browser, and WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming needs application/wasm.
# The page's CSP (above) is unchanged and applies to the worker too: measured
# on 2026-10-04, Pyodide 314.0.7 loads and runs under it without unsafe-eval.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/wasm/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/wasm/*.mjs
  Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8

/wasm/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm

/wasm/*.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

/wasm/*.zip
  Content-Type: application/zip

/workers/*.mjs
  Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8
